### The 6 Core Distinctions: Constructor vs Method

| Feature | Constructor | Method |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Purpose** | Initializes state for a new object | Performs an operation or behavior |
| **Return Type** | **NO return type** (not even `void`) | Must declare a return type (or `void`) |
| **Name** | Must match the class name exactly | Any valid identifier (camelCase) |
| **Invocation** | Invoked automatically via `new` | Invoked explicitly via reference (`obj.method()`) |
| **Frequency** | Executes **once per object lifecycle** | Can execute **many times** on demand |
| **Compiler Default** | Provided if zero constructors written | Never created automatically |

---

### The Famous Trap: The 'void Constructor' Trick Question

Consider this common technical interview trap:
```java
class Student {
    // TRAP: Has a return type 'void'!
    void Student() {
        System.out.println("This is NOT a constructor!");
    }
}
```

Because **`void Student()`** declares a return type, the Java compiler treats it as a standard instance method that happens to share the class name! It is **NOT** a constructor.

---

### Demonstrating the Distinctions in Code

```java
class AccountManager {
    String accountId;

    // 1. Constructor: Initializes instance on creation
    AccountManager(String id) {
        this.accountId = id;
        System.out.println("Constructor: AccountManager initialized with ID " + accountId);
    }

    // 2. Method: Performs repeatable operational behavior
    void auditAccount() {
        System.out.println("Method: Auditing transactions for account " + accountId);
    }
}

public class ConstructorVsMethodDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        AccountManager manager = new AccountManager("ACC-77"); // Constructor runs once
        manager.auditAccount(); // Method runs on demand
        manager.auditAccount(); // Method runs again
    }
}
```

**Console Output:**
```text
Constructor: AccountManager initialized with ID ACC-77
Method: Auditing transactions for account ACC-77
Method: Auditing transactions for account ACC-77
```

